  <h2>When the career you built stops fitting</h2>
  <p>There is a particular conversation that happens a lot in Christy's room, and it usually starts with an apology. The person is doing well. They know they are doing well. They have the job, or the income, or the title they were working toward, and they feel faintly ridiculous sitting in a therapist's office talking about it.</p>
  <p>But a life is not a spreadsheet, and things that look good on paper can be quietly the wrong shape. Sometimes the ambition was inherited rather than chosen. Sometimes the work that used to feel like yours became a role you perform. And sometimes you just grew, and the plan did not grow with you, which is nobody's failure and still has to be dealt with.</p>
  <p>Christy came to therapy after two other careers, in television production and in consumer sales analysis, so she has been on the inside of ambitious industries. She is not going to tell you to want less. The more useful question is which parts of what you want are actually yours.</p>
{% endcall %}
